<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="cd752705">&lt;p&gt;
    Project communications focuses on key messages from the perspective of the project/engagement team to the external
    stakeholders.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Project Communications refers to the formal, packaged, or structured information disseminated by the project team or
    received from client stakeholders. These communications are managed by the project team so that project stakeholders
    have information at agreed upon times through agreed upon channels. Effective communications to all stakeholders is a
    critical success factor in project management. This work product represents the formal, packaged or structured
    information disseminated by the project team or that received from client stakeholders and managed or consumed by the
    project team.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    It is important to plan the communications activities, methods, and channels so that communications are efficient and
    effective. Communications are also a two-way proposition. Collecting feedback is often as important as providing
    information from the perspective of the client sponsor or engagement team. Stakeholders across the organization have
    diverse views of the business, and of change and the role it plays. By making stakeholders aware of this diversity,
    communication can show them new perspectives and the case for change.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Internal communications, such as status reporting, risk and issue tracking, and scope management should also be
    considered part of the overall communications approach for the engagement.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Project Stakeholder Communications&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img style=&quot;HEIGHT: 349px; WIDTH: 616px&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/concepts/./resources/proj_stakeholder_comm.png&quot; width=&quot;718&quot;
    height=&quot;405&quot; /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Why Communication is Important&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Communication is a primary change management tool for any project and key to reinforcing desired behaviors. The overall
    objective of communication is to create a two way flow of information and to use the dialogue to channel efforts in the
    right direction until the objectives of the project have been achieved. Communication is required through the life of
    the project/program and will vary in its nature depending on the point in the life cycle. The aim is to confirm that
    the right people have the right information at the right time. Communication strategy is developed for all
    communications early in the engagement/project cycle. A variety of methods are utilized to confirm that all audiences
    and stakeholders remain informed and involved in the project.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Client communications is one of the key drivers of EY Service Quality that advance our vision of differentiation based
    on quality. It is one aspect of our service that we believe to be most important in setting EY apart. Effective
    communications management increases the client organization’s understanding, ownership and acceptance of change that is
    being introduced.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
     &lt;strong&gt;How to Communicate&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The channel in which communication occurs along with the size of the audience is important to consider when delivering
    or desiring certain kinds of messages or behaviors.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/concepts/./resources/proj_comm.png&quot; width=&quot;426&quot; height=&quot;205&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Communication Best Practices&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;u&gt;Effectiveness&lt;/u&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Visible support - Not “do as I say”
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Confirm understanding - Not just awareness
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Manage expectations actively - Not passively
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Be receptive - Not defensive
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Surface concerns - Not suppress them
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Tailor communications - not one size fits all
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Create demand - Use the grapevine - Not try to control it
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;u&gt;Efficiency&lt;/u&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Set up email group/s for your project to cover key stakeholders. However, be aware of the limitations of e-mail in
        conveying information (especially “broadcast” ones)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Schedule regular review meetings that will exist for the life of the project and include stakeholders or their
        representatives
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Create document templates (change requests form, status report, meeting agenda and minutes)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        When communicating, announce the topic, background, next steps and list issues (this will stop some questions
        dead). Follow up with the detailed plans and progress; having had prior warning, people will have had the chance to
        give the information preliminary thought.&lt;br /&gt;
        &lt;br /&gt;
        &lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
